Article I, Section 8 lists ("enumerates") the powers of Congress, including to:
lay and collect taxes, and borrow money;
regulate commerce with foreign nations, among the states, and with Indian tribes;
coin money; establish post offices; set rules for naturalization (becoming a citizen);
declare war; raise and support armies; provide and maintain a navy;
create federal courts below the Supreme Court;
make all laws "necessary and proper" for carrying out these powers (the Elastic Clause), which is the basis of implied powers.
Congress can also propose constitutional amendments, override a presidential veto with a two-thirds vote of both houses, and impeach and remove officials.

Ratified 1791 as part of the Bill of Rights. It provides:
a grand jury indictment for serious federal crimes (except in military cases);
no double jeopardy (being tried twice for the same offense);
no person can be compelled to be a witness against himself (the right against self-incrimination, "pleading the Fifth");
no one may be deprived of life, liberty, or property without due process of law;
private property may not be taken for public use without just compensation (eminent domain).
Miranda v. Arizona (1966) requires police to warn suspects in custody of these rights before questioning.

Ratified 1791 as part of the Bill of Rights. In criminal prosecutions, the accused has the right to:
a speedy and public trial by an impartial jury of the state and district where the crime was committed;
be informed of the nature and cause of the accusation;
be confronted with the witnesses against him;
compulsory process (subpoenas) to obtain favorable witnesses;
the assistance of counsel (a lawyer). Gideon v. Wainwright (1963) held that states must provide a lawyer to defendants in felony cases who cannot afford one.

Collision theory: particles react only when they collide with enough energy (at least the activation energy, Eₐ) and the correct orientation.
Rate increases with:
higher concentration (or gas pressure) → more frequent collisions;
higher temperature → faster particles, and a larger fraction have energy ≥ Eₐ;
greater surface area (smaller pieces, powders) → more contact;
a catalyst → provides a pathway with lower activation energy.
A catalyst is not used up, does not change ΔH, and does not change the position of equilibrium (it speeds up forward and reverse reactions equally). Enzymes are biological catalysts.

The cytoskeleton is a network of protein fibres in the cytoplasm that gives the cell its shape, anchors organelles and helps the cell and its contents move.
Three types:
microfilaments (thinnest, made of actin) — cell movement, muscle contraction, cytokinesis in animal cells;
intermediate filaments — mechanical strength;
microtubules (thickest, hollow tubes of tubulin) — tracks for moving vesicles, form the spindle fibres in cell division and the core of cilia and flagella.

Bulk transport moves large particles or large amounts of material in vesicles; it uses energy.
Endocytosis: the membrane folds inward to bring material INTO the cell.
phagocytosis (cell eating) — engulfing large particles or whole cells, e.g. a white blood cell engulfing a bacterium;
pinocytosis (cell drinking) — taking in droplets of fluid;
receptor-mediated endocytosis — taking in specific molecules that bind receptors (e.g. cholesterol carried in LDL).
Exocytosis: a vesicle fuses with the membrane to release material OUT of the cell, e.g. secreting hormones, digestive enzymes or neurotransmitters.

Meiosis makes gametes (sperm and eggs) with half the chromosome number: diploid (2n) → haploid (n). In humans 46 → 23.
Meiosis I (separates homologous chromosomes):
Prophase I — homologous chromosomes pair up (synapsis) into tetrads, and crossing over swaps DNA between them;
Metaphase I — homologous PAIRS line up at the middle; their random orientation is independent assortment;
Anaphase I — homologs separate to opposite poles (sister chromatids stay together);
Telophase I and cytokinesis → 2 haploid cells.
Meiosis II (separates sister chromatids, like mitosis): prophase II, metaphase II, anaphase II, telophase II.
Result: 4 genetically different haploid cells.
Sources of variation: crossing over, independent assortment, and random fertilization.

A mineral is a naturally occurring, inorganic solid with a definite chemical composition and an orderly crystal structure. Rocks are mixtures of one or more minerals (or other materials).
Identification properties:
hardness (Mohs scale);
lustre — how the surface reflects light (metallic or non-metallic, e.g. glassy, pearly, dull);
streak — the colour of its powder on an unglazed porcelain plate (more reliable than colour; hematite has a reddish-brown streak);
colour (least reliable — many minerals come in several colours);
cleavage (breaks along flat planes, e.g. mica) or fracture (breaks unevenly or with curved surfaces, e.g. quartz);
density, magnetism (magnetite), reaction with acid (calcite fizzes).
Silicates (containing silicon and oxygen) are the most common mineral group; feldspars are the most abundant minerals in the crust.
